 ## Subtopics
 
-### GPU-based Raycasting Rendering
-A variant focusing on the general implementation of raycasting on GPU architectures, emphasizing the use of fragment shaders for parallel processing of rays [S1, unofficial]. It differs from CPU-based approaches by offloading the heavy computation of ray traversal and compositing to the GPU's parallel cores [S1, unofficial]. It is chosen when real-time performance is required for large volumetric datasets [S1, unofficial].
+### [GPU-based Raycasting Rendering](14516-gpu-based-raycasting-rendering.md)
+GPU-based raycasting renders volumetric datasets by casting rays from the camera through the volume on the GPU, accumulating color and opacity along each ray path. It differs from CPU-based raycasting by leveraging massive parallelism to achieve real-time frame rates, which is mandatory for high-refresh-rate displays like the HTC Vive (90 Hz). It builds on the [GPU Ray Casting Pipeline](14281-gpu-ray-casting-pipeline.md) by offloading the ray traversal and compositing steps to the graphics hardware.
+
+### [GPU Raycasting for Stack-Based Terrains](15741-gpu-raycasting-for-stack-based-terrains.md)
+GPU Raycasting for Stack-Based Terrains (SBRT) is a direct volume rendering technique adapted for geoscientific terrain data, implemented on the GPU to enable real-time visualization. It solves the problem of efficiently rendering complex, layered subsurface structures by casting rays from a virtual camera through each output buffer pixel and composing sampled colors along the ray path. What sets it apart from standard raycasting is its specific adaptation to the SBRT data structure, which uses a stack-based representation of geological intervals, and its hybrid deferred strategy for normal calculation to avoid the performance bottlenecks associated with 3D convolution on sparse stack data.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of pixels and the number of samples per ray ($O(P \cdot S)$), where $P$ is the number of pixels and $S$ is the number of samples along the ray [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the volume of the 3D texture ($O(V)$) [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-*   **Strengths:** High parallelism on GPU; efficient texture sampling; real-time potential [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Memory bandwidth bound for large volumes; fixed step size may lead to aliasing or inefficiency [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** Real-time visualization of CT/MRI data [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When adaptive step sizes or complex ray marching logic is required beyond simple texture sampling [S1, unofficial].
+Not given in the sources.
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from the basic rendering pipeline for visualizing volume models using a GPU version of the ray casting process [S1, unofficial]. It was proposed in the context of a thesis on enhanced perception in volume visualization, aiming to familiarize readers with different topics in visualization [S1, unofficial]. The approach uses the front-to-back compositing scheme and is based on volumetric data sets from CT and MRI images [S1, unofficial]. It is used in medical visualization and scientific visualization [S1, unofficial].
+The basic rendering pipeline described here is intended to familiarize the reader with the different topics that appear in the rest of the thesis [S1, unofficial]. The methods proposed are based on the ray casting algorithm using the front-to-back compositing scheme [S1, unofficial]. The approaches presented in this thesis propose different modifications to this basic pipeline in order to obtain the desired results [S1, unofficial]. For a detailed overview on Visualization, recommended books include *The Visualization Handbook* edited by Charles D. Hansen and Christopher R. Johnson [36], *Visualization in Medicine* by Bernhard Preim and Dirk Bartz [82], and *Real-Time Volume Graphics* by Klaus Engel, Markus Hadwiger, Joe M. Kniss, Christoph Rezk-Salama and Daniel Weiskopf [34], which also presents technical details for the implementation of different visualization methods [S1, unofficial].
 
